   "source": [
    "# Syndrome Cycles\n",
    "\n",
    "In this notebook, we implement a full **quantum error correction (QEC)** cycle using the 3-qubit **bit-flip code**.\n",
    "\n",
    "We will:\n",
    "1. **Encode** a logical qubit  \n",
    "2. **Apply** a single-qubit error  \n",
    "3. **Measure** the syndrome using stabilizers  \n",
    "4. **Recover** the correct state  \n",
    "5. **Decode** back to the logical qubit and compare\n",
    "\n",
    "This cycle demonstrates the entire **feedback logic** behind QEC."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "6f03c62b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### ❓ Why this matters\n",
    "\n",
    "Most students learn QEC as matrix multiplication.  \n",
    "Noro teaches it as **a system of checks, structure, and recovery.**\n",
    "\n",
    "This cycle is the first minimal model of **resilient thinking.**"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e55f318f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### ✅ Requirements\n",
    "\n",
    "- Python 3.8+  \n",
    "- `numpy`  \n",
    "- Optional: `openqstack` (for future extensions)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dcca82ad",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 🧠 Goal\n",
    "\n",
    "By the end of this notebook, you will understand:\n",
    "\n",
    "- What it means to **stabilize** a quantum state  \n",
    "- How **syndromes** identify broken structure  \n",
    "- How to **recover** a logical bit from noisy components"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c91a1083",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "> “A stabilizer is a rule that a good state obeys.  \n",
    "> An error breaks the rule.  \n",
    "> The syndrome tells us which rule failed.  \n",
    "> Recovery brings the structure back.”"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "33b1df3e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Let's begin with imports and state encoding."
   ]
  }
